00:18:59,000 --> 00:19:01,980
with the scenarios is a good idea

382
00:19:01,980 --> 00:19:05,720
because they tend to either make them insanely clever,

383
00:19:05,720 --> 00:19:09,200
or that someone was like attacked by ninjas

384
00:19:09,200 --> 00:19:10,960
and fell out of a helicopter or something

385
00:19:10,960 --> 00:19:13,760
and you know it's not you kind of need.

386
00:19:13,760 --> 00:19:15,280
it's an instructional thing.

387
00:19:15,280 --> 00:19:18,680
It needs to have just the right level of challenge.

388
00:19:18,680 --> 00:19:20,040
The whole setting it up,

389
00:19:21,160 --> 00:19:23,560
especially with the fake wounds, and then you know

390
00:19:23,560 --> 00:19:26,800
people, it's a great thing to get good at.

391
00:19:26,800 --> 00:19:28,800
Yeah, I was thinking the other day

392
00:19:28,800 --> 00:19:32,879
I guess some people are risk managers,

393
00:19:32,879 --> 00:19:35,080
some people are risk averse

394
00:19:35,080 --> 00:19:38,000
and then some people are just risk ignore,

395
00:19:39,200 --> 00:19:42,800
which, along the way, might have been me occasionally

396
00:19:42,840 --> 00:19:44,159
And for some reason,

397
00:19:44,159 --> 00:19:48,040
we always had all the right stuff there,

398
00:19:48,040 --> 00:19:53,040
but it occasionally almost seemed like an accident.

399
00:19:53,280 --> 00:19:57,760
I think that it was an artifact

400
00:19:57,760 --> 00:20:00,020
of actually doing the program

401
00:20:00,020 --> 00:20:02,800
and having first aid and first aid merit badge

402
00:20:02,800 --> 00:20:06,800
and safety- and you know, back in my day,

403
00:20:06,800 --> 00:20:08,680
safety merit badge and stuff-

404
00:20:08,680 --> 00:20:12,200
we just ended up with enough stuff

405
00:20:12,320 --> 00:20:13,680
and we didn't even know about it-

406
00:20:13,680 --> 00:20:16,800
adults or scouts to some extent-

407
00:20:16,800 --> 00:20:19,240
because I don't think of myself

408
00:20:19,240 --> 00:20:23,480
as a risk manager very much or very well,

409
00:20:23,480 --> 00:20:25,560
And maybe I just do it in the back of my head-

410
00:20:25,560 --> 00:20:29,040
and enough that we haven't had any disasters.

411
00:20:30,280 --> 00:20:32,240
Well, you know, I like what you said about

412
00:20:32,240 --> 00:20:35,600
you know, when things have happened on your trips,

413
00:20:35,600 --> 00:20:38,480
that you already kind of knew what to do.

414
00:20:38,480 --> 00:20:41,280
You had this stuff and you had,

415
00:20:41,320 --> 00:20:44,000
because really being prepared

416
00:20:44,000 --> 00:20:48,200
is not about hauling huge amounts of stuff,

417
00:20:48,200 --> 00:20:50,480
It's about what's in your head

418
00:20:50,480 --> 00:20:54,080
and what's kind of in your fingers, that you've practiced it,

419
00:20:54,080 --> 00:20:56,280
that you teach first aid every year

420
00:20:56,280 --> 00:21:00,040
and you learn that, relearn it every year that way.

421
00:21:00,040 --> 00:21:03,760
So, and that's what the emergency drills are about

422
00:21:03,760 --> 00:21:05,480
and that's you know those kind of things

423
00:21:05,480 --> 00:21:08,160
is that's why we talk on the radio every week

424
00:21:09,160 --> 00:21:10,840
with the volunteers,

425
00:21:10,840 --> 00:21:14,640
just so that when something happens you know what to do.

426
00:21:14,640 --> 00:21:16,960
I've been on the scene of a couple of road accidents.

427
00:21:16,960 --> 00:21:20,800
I've been there when people were trying to revive somebody

428
00:21:20,800 --> 00:21:25,960
with CPR and you know, instead of being one of the 20

429
00:21:25,960 --> 00:21:28,720
or 30 people who are standing there watching

430
00:21:28,720 --> 00:21:31,200
you, basically you know what to do.

431
00:21:31,200 --> 00:21:35,440
And a lot of that goes back to the way that we work

432
00:21:35,680 --> 00:21:38,600
in scouts, and I like what you said, Larry.

433
00:21:38,600 --> 00:21:41,000
you said it's an artifact of doing the program.

434
00:21:42,240 --> 00:21:46,680
What seems to be, I mean, it seems to come out of it most.

435
00:21:46,680 --> 00:21:49,000
most of the time we do okay, you know,

436
00:21:49,000 --> 00:21:51,560
and not that Larry is such a great

437
00:21:51,560 --> 00:21:53,360
and wonderful risk manager

438
00:21:53,360 --> 00:21:55,720
and took all of this into account ahead of time.

439
00:21:57,160 --> 00:21:59,360
You know, I can't really say that.

440
00:22:00,600 --> 00:22:03,680
I think it gets. it's part of the culture of scouting-

441
00:22:03,720 --> 00:22:08,720
Let's say it that way- that we do inherently risky activities

442
00:22:10,200 --> 00:22:15,200
and we have to be prepared to understand what those risks are

443
00:22:15,920 --> 00:22:19,040
and be able to respond to things that go wrong.

444
00:22:19,040 --> 00:22:23,960
And you know, if you've been around for a few years

445
00:22:23,960 --> 00:22:27,320
and you've gone through these things several different times,

446
00:22:27,320 --> 00:22:28,720
they're kind of second nature,

447
00:22:29,720 --> 00:22:31,920
An artifact of the program.

448
00:22:31,920 --> 00:22:34,640
I'm fascinated with the phrase.

449
00:22:34,640 --> 00:22:36,560
I think that's a very good way of saying it.

450
00:22:36,560 --> 00:22:39,320
On May 30th I had an event.

451
00:22:39,320 --> 00:22:42,600
The fire truck showed up in about two minutes

452
00:22:44,120 --> 00:22:45,800
and then the ambulance was there-

453
00:22:45,800 --> 00:22:47,600
I don't know, I guess- a minute later

454
00:22:47,600 --> 00:22:50,920
and those guys were just amazing

455
00:22:50,920 --> 00:22:52,360
And of course it was in the hospital

456
00:22:52,360 --> 00:22:54,160
where all the real work took place

457
00:22:54,160 --> 00:22:56,200
and they put a stand in my heart and the whole thing.

458
00:22:56,840 --> 00:22:59,960
I mean those guys. it was amazing how,

459
00:22:59,960 --> 00:23:02,380
but they just knew exactly what to do.

460
00:23:02,380 --> 00:23:04,560
My doctor said they saved my life.

461
00:23:04,560 --> 00:23:06,440
I guess I've had several scouts

462
00:23:06,440 --> 00:23:11,240
who have ended up as firemen and EMTs along the way

463
00:23:11,240 --> 00:23:16,240
and I think scouting does prepare not only the skills,

464
00:23:16,680 --> 00:23:20,160
but the mindset and I guess, if you wanna use

465
00:23:20,160 --> 00:23:23,280
that artifact of the program kind of idea,

466
00:23:23,280 --> 00:23:25,760
but I mean if you can be as prepared

467
00:23:25,960 --> 00:23:27,480
as these guys are, and of course

468
00:23:27,480 --> 00:23:29,840
they have all the equipment and all the stuff,

469
00:23:29,840 --> 00:23:32,680
but still they've trained, they knew what to do,

470
00:23:32,680 --> 00:23:34,240
They didn't just kind of stand there and go,

471
00:23:34,240 --> 00:23:36,280
they went right to work

472
00:23:36,280 --> 00:23:37,120
And I'm impressed,

473
00:23:37,120 --> 00:23:38,720
Yeah, they practice, practice, practice.

474
00:23:38,720 --> 00:23:41,000
If they haven't done it in a week they'll practice.

475
00:23:41,000 --> 00:23:45,440
Well, and I had something about: oh, four years ago

476
00:23:45,440 --> 00:23:50,360
we had a meeting of our company emergency response team

477
00:23:50,360 --> 00:23:53,600
and we did CPR, kind of just a refresher,

478
00:23:53,600 --> 00:23:57,640
and Heimlich and stuff. and that evening at dinner

479
00:23:57,640 --> 00:24:00,840
my older son choked on a piece of shrimp

480
00:24:00,840 --> 00:24:03,480
as in not breathing, choked.

481
00:24:03,480 --> 00:24:08,480
And so my other son, Scout, calls 911

482
00:24:09,260 --> 00:24:11,880
and I did the Heimlich and got it out.

483
00:24:11,880 --> 00:24:14,640
and you really have to honk on somebody

484
00:24:14,640 --> 00:24:17,400
to get that to work, I'll tell you that.

485
00:24:17,400 --> 00:24:22,200
But by the time they got there he was breathing again.

486
00:24:22,240 --> 00:24:24,640
That's what practice is for.

487
00:24:24,640 --> 00:24:27,080
There's a troop down the road 12 miles

488
00:24:27,080 --> 00:24:31,520
and the Scout master is on the eccentric side

489
00:24:31,520 --> 00:24:34,680
and he- they were doing a model rocket shoot. one day

490
00:24:34,680 --> 00:24:37,400
He built his own engine.

491
00:24:37,400 --> 00:24:39,880
The alarms, the alarms are going off right now.

492
00:24:39,880 --> 00:24:41,040
You better believe it.

493
00:24:41,040 --> 00:24:45,680
He touched the match to it and it blew up

494
00:24:45,680 --> 00:24:47,740
and a piece of shrapnel went through

495
00:24:47,740 --> 00:24:50,120
and cut his femoral artery.

496
00:24:50,600 --> 00:24:53,600
The Scouts- immediately all the Scouts around-

497
00:24:53,600 --> 00:24:55,720
saw that and they jumped on him,

498
00:24:55,720 --> 00:24:59,440
jumped on that thing and put pressure on it

499
00:24:59,440 --> 00:25:03,760
and they called 911, the ambulance finally got there.

500
00:25:03,760 --> 00:25:06,880
They told him that if the Scouts hadn't been there-

501
00:25:06,880 --> 00:25:09,760
you know, if they somebody else had been there

502
00:25:09,760 --> 00:25:11,000
that didn't know what to do,

503
00:25:11,000 --> 00:25:14,160
he'd have bled to death in just a few minutes.

504
00:25:14,160 --> 00:25:17,680
but the Scouts knew exactly what to do

505
00:25:17,720 --> 00:25:22,040
and how to do it and it happened just instantly

506
00:25:22,040 --> 00:25:24,200
and they saved his life.

507
00:25:24,200 --> 00:25:25,040
Wow,

508
00:25:25,040 --> 00:25:28,520
So, yeah, so we call him Rocket Man now.

509
00:25:28,520 --> 00:25:32,120
Ha, ha, ha, ha, ha, ha, ha, ha, ha, ha, ha, ha, ha, ha, ha, ha, ha, ha ha.

510
00:25:32,120 --> 00:25:36,920
But yeah, that was a scary time for him and you know

511
00:25:36,920 --> 00:25:40,520
You hear stories like that kind of regularly

512
00:25:40,520 --> 00:25:43,000
If you look for them. they're happening all the time.

513
00:25:43,000 --> 00:25:45,200
Somebody administers the Heimlich maneuver,

514
00:25:45,240 --> 00:25:47,440
somebody uses CPR, somebody uses

515
00:25:47,440 --> 00:25:50,920
that very basic first aid knowledge that they have,

516
00:25:50,920 --> 00:25:55,920
and you know they absolutely can save people's lives

517
00:25:56,360 --> 00:26:00,720
or prevent them from, you know, a greater injury.

518
00:26:00,720 --> 00:26:03,000
That is one of the things

519
00:26:03,000 --> 00:26:05,600
that is extraordinarily common

520
00:26:05,600 --> 00:26:08,000
from my experience in Scouting Circles.

521
00:26:08,000 --> 00:26:11,360
The BSA has the National Court of Honor

522
00:26:11,360 --> 00:26:14,880
that will present heroism awards to Scouts.

523
00:26:14,880 --> 00:26:17,280
They always had a feature in Boy's Life

524
00:26:17,280 --> 00:26:20,000
about a heroism award that had been issued

525
00:26:20,000 --> 00:26:21,040
Scouts in action.

526
00:26:21,040 --> 00:26:23,280
I always turn to Scouts in action first.

527
00:26:23,280 --> 00:26:26,000
It's interesting. I feel like those are probably

528
00:26:26,000 --> 00:26:28,560
under awarded in a lot of councils.

529
00:26:28,560 --> 00:26:31,400
I don't think people are quite aware of those

530
00:26:31,400 --> 00:26:35,120
because, yeah, there's three levels or something on that.

531
00:26:35,120 --> 00:26:39,280
The highest level is an honor medal with crossed palms

532
00:26:39,280 --> 00:26:43,520
and that is a scout that's demonstrated unusual heroism

533
00:26:43,520 --> 00:26:47,940
or extraordinary skill or resourcefulness

534
00:26:47,940 --> 00:26:49,879
in saving or attempting to save a life

535
00:26:49,879 --> 00:26:52,240
at extreme risk to themselves.

536
00:26:52,240 --> 00:26:56,480
And then there are several different levels of honor medal.

537
00:26:56,480 --> 00:26:59,040
There's a national certificate of merit

538
00:26:59,040 --> 00:27:01,240
and that is for someone who has performed

539
00:27:01,240 --> 00:27:02,920
a significant act of service

540
00:27:02,920 --> 00:27:05,440
that is deserving of national recognition.

541
00:27:05,440 --> 00:27:07,800
And then I think what you were referring to, Walter,

542
00:27:07,800 --> 00:27:11,800
is something called the local council certificate of merit,

543
00:27:11,800 --> 00:27:14,200
And this situation does not meet the criteria

544
00:27:14,200 --> 00:27:16,000
for the National Court of Honor Award

545
00:27:16,000 --> 00:27:18,399
but is an act deserving of recognition.

546
00:27:18,399 --> 00:27:21,240
I had several years ago, two boys

547
00:27:21,240 --> 00:27:23,240
within the course of a year or two

548
00:27:23,240 --> 00:27:24,840
who administered the Heimlich maneuver

549
00:27:24,840 --> 00:27:27,000
to a family member, one to a younger brother

550
00:27:27,000 --> 00:27:28,399
and one to a grandfather.

551
00:27:28,399 --> 00:27:31,440
I was made aware of that and I did file

552
00:27:31,440 --> 00:27:33,720
for a local council certificate of merit

553
00:27:33,720 --> 00:27:36,399
because I thought it met that criteria

554
00:27:36,399 --> 00:27:38,200
and we were able to present those to them.

555
00:27:38,200 --> 00:27:39,639
So, Walter, I think you're right.

556
00:27:39,640 --> 00:27:42,320
I think these things are kind of under rewarded

557
00:27:42,320 --> 00:27:44,560
because I don't know that everyone is aware

558
00:27:44,560 --> 00:27:45,840
that they're available.

559
00:27:45,840 --> 00:27:47,800
Well, and I think the Medal of Merit

560
00:27:47,800 --> 00:27:51,760
is one that's. some acts of meritorious service above and beyond

561
00:27:53,080 --> 00:27:56,160
may involve life-saving. it's not confined to life-saving

562
00:27:56,160 --> 00:27:58,920
And so- but you know, Heimlich maneuver

563
00:27:58,920 --> 00:28:00,240
or something like that,

564
00:28:00,240 --> 00:28:05,240
it's can be awarded for any meritorious action

565
00:28:06,720 --> 00:28:09,200
using some aspect of scouting skills or learning.

566
00:28:10,160 --> 00:28:11,800
Well, there you go.

567
00:28:12,640 --> 00:28:15,240
So you know that's.

568
00:28:15,240 --> 00:28:18,600
I think we should recognize scouts and scouts

569
00:28:18,600 --> 00:28:19,840
for doing this kind of thing.

570
00:28:19,840 --> 00:28:21,400
I think it's an important aspect

571
00:28:21,400 --> 00:28:25,080
of helping everybody understand, you know,

572
00:28:25,080 --> 00:28:27,320
what a contribution it makes to the community

573
00:28:27,320 --> 00:28:30,200
when you're actually prepared for something,

574
00:28:30,200 --> 00:28:33,280
And you may be more prepared than you realize.

575
00:28:33,280 --> 00:28:34,120
Hope so,

576
00:28:34,120 --> 00:28:34,960
Hope so.

577
00:28:36,880 --> 00:28:39,080
Well, you always feel a lot less prepared

578
00:28:39,159 --> 00:28:40,280
than you would like to be,

579
00:28:40,280 --> 00:28:43,480
but you may be the best prepared person in the room

580
00:28:43,480 --> 00:28:47,120
for the first hundred or so scouts that cut their fingers.

581
00:28:47,120 --> 00:28:49,520
You just, you know, begin to get good at it.

582
00:28:52,520 --> 00:28:56,520
I'm up to 287. right now

583
00:28:56,520 --> 00:28:58,240
There could be award, for

584
00:28:58,240 --> 00:29:00,399
you could have like an extra little scout

585
00:29:00,399 --> 00:29:03,000
you know device on that, one for each one,

586
00:29:03,000 --> 00:29:04,439
An Oakleaf cluster

587
00:29:04,439 --> 00:29:05,280
With Band-Aid,

588
00:29:05,280 --> 00:29:08,720
With Band-Aid for a minimum of two stitches.

589
00:29:08,760 --> 00:29:11,120
I think we covered what I was looking to cover

590
